// Runtime/SlabArena.maxon — ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE COMPILER STILL OWNS OF THE PAGE LAYER: THE GEOMETRY, THE TWO
// `.data` WORDS, THE FAMILY BAND, AND THE ONE ENTRY POINT THAT IS STILL BUILT AS IR.**
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE EIGHT ENTRY POINTS ARE `runtime/SlabArena.maxon` SOURCE.** `__slab_assert_os_alloc`,
// `__slab_arena_new`, `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`, `__slab_arena_free_chunks`, `__slab_arena_of`,
// `__slab_arena_scavenge`, `__slab_arena_map_ensure` and `__slab_arena_map_set` are written over the
// `__Raw` floor and arrive through the ordinary pipeline. ⛔ **THE NINTH — `__slab_arena_map_get` — STAYS,
// and its staying is not debt.** `__slab_free` splices the walk INLINE (`emitSlabArenaMapGet`), because a
// frame around four loads and three tests is pure overhead on the path of every free in the language; the
// framed twin here is `__slab_drain_remote`'s. Porting it would be a SECOND spelling of one walk.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE ARENA'S BODIES ARE REACHED BY CALLS THE COMPILER MINTS, SO THE REACH IS DECLARED RATHER THAN
// DISCOVERED** — `StdlibSource.runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled`, which is what stops the from-`main` walk
// filing a body the installers below will call as `unreachedRuntimeTier`. ⚠ It credits REACH and nothing
// else: the `.data` words below are laid out where a surviving arena body names them, so a program that
// cannot allocate carries neither.
//
// What follows is the PAGE LAYER's design, which decides the geometry this file checks and the tier file
// writes: a bitmap-managed ARENA committed a granule at a time, a singly-linked list of them, and the
// pointer→span reverse map a `free` needs to answer *"which span owns this address?"* without a header word
// on every object.
//
// ⚠ **THE REVERSE MAP IS NOT GO'S SHAPE, AND THE TWO ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.** Go's `mheap.arenas`
// is a TWO-level map over ARENA numbers (`arenaL1Bits`/`arenaL2Bits`, `malloc.go:279`), reaching per-page
// metadata through the `heapArena` record it finds. This is a THREE-level map over the PAGE index, reaching
// the span pointer directly. Go's is the better structure once an arena carries per-page metadata worth
// grouping; ours carries none yet, so the extra indirection would buy a level and pay for a record.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHERE A RESERVATION IS FREE, THE ARENA IS 64 MiB OF RESERVED ADDRESS SPACE AND 64 KiB OF COMMITTED
// MEMORY, AND THOSE ARE TWO DIFFERENT NUMBERS.** `osAllocPages` reserves and commits together on every
// lane, so an arena mapped through it would make a hello-world resident in 64 MiB where the bump allocator
// is resident in 64 KiB. `StdOp.osReservePages`/`StdOp.osCommitPages` separate the two, so the arena is
// Go's shape: `__slab_arena_new` RESERVES the whole 64 MiB, commits the first `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`
// of it, and `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` commits further granules as the first-fit scan reaches them. A
// program that allocates one box is resident in one granule; the other 63.9 MiB costs address space, which
// Windows does not charge and Linux does not back.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THAT SIZE IS NOT PICKED, IT IS THE REVERSE MAP'S BOTTOM LEVEL — WHICH IS WHAT THE 2 MiB ARENA COST US.**
// An L3 table describes `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages, i.e. 64 MiB of address space, and it is itself allocated
// out of an arena. A 2 MiB arena landing in a 64 MiB region of its own therefore spent a 64 KiB table to
// describe 2 MiB of heap — **3.1%**. At 64 MiB the arena and the table describe the same span, so the cost is
// 8 chunks in 8192 (**0.1%**) when an arena starts on a 64 MiB boundary and 16 in 8192 (**0.2%**) when it
// straddles two, which is the honest range rather than a fixed figure. See `ReservedArenaChunkCount`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND ON wasm32-wasi EVERY WORD OF THAT IS FALSE, WHICH IS WHY THE ARENA HAS TWO GEOMETRIES.** Linear
// memory has no reservation: `memory.grow` takes the range AND backs it, so `osReservePages` IS
// `osAllocPages` there (`StdToWasm.emitOsAllocPages` is the emitter BOTH ops route to, and its commit twin
// answers the address it was handed), and the "free" 63.9 MiB is 63.9 MiB of charged linear memory.
// **MEASURED with `scripts/peak-rss.sh --repeat 3`, two compilers built from ONE tree differing in ONE
// constant, run under `vendor/wasmtime`:**
//
//   | program                        | 64 MiB arena | 512 KiB arena |
//   |--------------------------------|-------------:|--------------:|
//   | heap-touching hello-world      |    70.32 MiB |    **5.22 MiB** |
//   | 32,768 live interpolated Strings |  67.36 MiB |   **11.10 MiB** |
//
// It was not a rounding error on that lane; at hello-world size it WAS the whole program — and the second
// row is what says the small arena has not merely moved the cost, because a heap 32k objects deep still
// fits in 11 MiB. The native lanes' emitted reservation is untouched at 67108864.
//
// ⭐⭐ **GO ANSWERS THIS WITH A PER-PLATFORM CONSTANT, AND HERE ONE OF ITS ROWS IS TAKEN AND ANOTHER IS
// DELIBERATELY NOT — WHICH IS DECIDED BY THE REASON, NOT BY THE NUMBER.** `logHeapArenaBytes`
// (`vendor/go/src/runtime/malloc.go:259`) is `(6+20)` = 64 MiB on 64-bit non-Windows-non-wasm-non-iOS,
// `(2+20)` = 4 MiB on 64-bit Windows, 32-bit and iOS/arm64, and `(9+10)` = **512 KiB on wasm**. Go states
// both reasons out loud at `:238`: Windows *"because all committed memory is charged to the process, even if
// it's not touched"*, and wasm *"because some Wasm programs have very small heap, and everything in the Wasm
// linear memory is charged."*
//
// **The wasm reason transfers exactly and the Windows one does not.** What Go commits per arena is its
// `heapArena` record, whose bitmap and span array are PROPORTIONAL to the arena, so Windows' commit charge
// bites it at 64 MiB. the compiler's per-arena eager commit is `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`, a CONSTANT — the
// bitmap of a 64 MiB arena is 128 words, so all of this file's metadata is 1 KiB inside a single chunk
// however large the arena grows. Windows therefore charges the compiler for one granule, and copying Go's 4 MiB
// there would be a number taken without the fact that produced it.
//
// ⇒ The lane is asked as **`IrModule.hasPageReservation`**, which is the MECHANISM and not the target: what
// this file needs to know is whether a reservation is free, and a field named `isWasm` would have had to be
// re-read by every later consumer that meant something else by it. `Compiler.maxon` sets it beside
// `debugStream`, at the seam where lowering has finished and nothing has been installed yet.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARENA IS ALIGNED TO A COMMIT GRANULE (W215), AND THE ARENA-SIZED ALIGNMENT GO USES IS A DIFFERENT
// THING THAT IS STILL DELIBERATELY NOT COPIED.** `__slab_arena_new` raises the base `osReservePages` answered
// to the next `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`, because the OS promises only its own page granularity and
// x64-linux's is 4 KiB — HALF a chunk, which puts the chunk grid out of phase with the reverse map's page
// grid. See that function for the whole failure and for what it measured; `arenaAlignSlackBytes` records why
// the slack it needs is free on the lanes that pay for it and zero on the lane that does not. What follows
// is about the OTHER alignment — to the ARENA's own 64 MiB — whose slack is a whole second arena.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE PRICE OF COPYING THAT ONE IS MEASURED.**
// `sysReserveAligned` (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mem.go:185`) over-reserves `size + align` and then either TRIMS
// the unaligned ends (POSIX) or — since Windows cannot release part of a reservation — releases the whole
// thing and re-reserves at the aligned address, retrying on a race. Two things stop that here, and neither is
// effort: `osReservePages` takes a SIZE and no address hint, so the Windows half of Go's routine cannot be
// spelled at all; and a trim cannot be emitted from this tier, because `osFreePages` means *release the whole
// reservation* on Windows and *unmap this sub-range* on POSIX, and a target-neutral body cannot ask for one
// and not the other. What it would buy is 0.2% → 0.1% of the map, plus `arenaBase == addr and -size`
// — a THIRD answer to the "owning arena's base" question below, and the cheapest of the three. What it would
// cost is a `size`-sized second reservation per arena, which on wasm is not address space at all but a second
// arena's worth of charged memory — a DOUBLING of the lane the geometry above exists to shrink, where the
// granule-sized alignment above costs that lane nothing at all. ⇒
// **FOLLOW-UP, NAMED: an address-hint operand on `osReservePages`** is what makes Go's routine expressible;
// until something needs the mask, a straddling arena costs one extra 64 KiB table.
//
// ⚠ **THE CHUNK IS `SlabPageBytes` — GO'S 8 KiB PAGE — AND IT IS NOT SPELLED HERE.** `SlabClasses.maxon`
// declares `SlabPageShift`/`SlabPageBytes` and `SlabClassTable.checkSlabClassTable` already asserts the two
// agree; this file reads them. Spelling the arena-map index math's own page masks as literals would be
// 8192 written twice, which is how a page-size change silently leaves the map indexing one geometry and
// the class table another.
//
// ✅ **NO LOCK OF ITS OWN, BECAUSE THE CALLER HOLDS ONE.** The whole slow path is locked, allocator and
// arena together, at the point green threads can run these on more than one OS thread: `SlabRuntime` holds
// `__slab_lock` across every call into this file on the allocate and release paths — `__slab_refill` takes
// it before `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` and `__slab_arena_map_set` and releases it after installing the
// span, `__slab_scavenge` holds it from its entry through `__slab_arena_scavenge`, and `__slab_meta_alloc`
// and `__slab_span_destroy` state the same requirement as a documented precondition rather than
// re-acquiring it.
//
// ⚠ **ONE ENTRY POINT IS REACHED WITHOUT THE LOCK: `__slab_arena_map_get`, from
// `SlabRuntime.__slab_drain_remote`'s walk**, which is a cross-P path and therefore exactly where a second M
// is. It is a READ of the reverse map, and it is named here because "the caller holds the lock" is the
// sentence above and this is its one exception — not because the exception has been shown to be safe. It
// has not been measured either way.
//
// ⚠ **AND THERE IS NO `__slab_init` / `__slab_init_done` HERE FOR A SECOND REASON THAT SURVIVES THE LOCK.**
// The `.data` image IS the initialization, exactly as `MmRuntime`'s slab cursor's zero means "no arena yet":
// `__slab_arena_list` starts 0, and the first `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` reads 0, maps an arena and
// retries. There is no state that can be observed before it is established, so there is nothing to guard.
//
// ✅ **THE OWNING ARENA'S BASE IS ASKED FOR RATHER THAN STORED.**
// `__slab_arena_free_chunks` needs an arena base its caller does not hold, and three of the four ways to
// supply one RECORD it at the moment a chunk run is handed out: a real out-value on the alloc entry point,
// an arena level in the reverse map, or an arena-ALIGNED reservation the address could be masked to.
// `__slab_arena_of` is the fourth and it stores nothing, on the observation that decides between them —
// **the question is asked at DESTRUCTION and would have been answered at BIRTH**, and a span is destroyed
// at most once and created at least once. See that function for the full argument.
//
// ⚠ The masked form is still the cheapest of all four and is still unreachable for the reason above: the
// header's own note on `sysReserveAligned` records what an address-hint operand on `osReservePages` would
// buy and what it would cost on the lane with no reservation.

// --- Geometry ---
//
// ⭐ **ONE NUMBER IS CHOSEN PER LANE AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS DERIVED FROM IT.** The bitmap's width, the byte
// count and the largest run a caller may ask for all follow from the chunk count. Stated the other way round
// — a picked bitmap width beside a picked size — the two would be numbers kept in agreement by hand, which is
// exactly how the 2 MiB arena came to cost 3.1% of itself in map tables. Written as a DERIVATION there is no
// second number to disagree, so what is left to assert is that the derivation LOSES NOTHING on EITHER lane —
// `checkSlabArenaGeometry`. (It is a shift and not a division because `/` at this tier answers a float, and
// the shift's exactness is the thing being asserted anyway.)

// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVING LANE'S ARENA IS NOT PICKED EITHER — IT IS ONE BOTTOM-LEVEL MAP TABLE'S SPAN.** An L3
// table describes `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages, so one arena is exactly one table's worth of address space
// and the map costs 0.1–0.2% of the heap it describes. Written as the identity rather than as 8192, the two
// cannot drift.
let ReservedArenaChunkCount = ArenaMapLevelEntries

// ⭐⭐ **512 KiB WHERE A RESERVATION IS AN ALLOCATION — GO'S OWN wasm ROW, AND IT BREAKS THE COUPLING ABOVE
// ON PURPOSE.** 64 chunks of 8 KiB is `logHeapArenaBytes = (9+10)` at `vendor/go/src/runtime/malloc.go:259`,
// and it is the whole of the 70.32 → 5.22 MiB collapse the header measures. The 1:1 arena↔table match is
// gone at this size — an L3 table still describes 64 MiB of address space while only 512 KiB of it is arena —
// and the naive reading of that is a 12.5% map overhead.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ~0.1% INSTEAD, AND THE REASON IS A PROPERTY OF THE LANE THAT HAS NO RESERVATION IN THE FIRST
// PLACE.** Linear memory is a SINGLE CONTIGUOUS REGION that only ever grows at the top: `memory.grow` hands
// back the old end, so arena N+1 begins exactly where arena N stops. Successive arenas are therefore
// ADJACENT, and ~128 of them fall inside one L3 table's span — the table is allocated once and shared, not
// once per arena. The 12.5% figure is what SCATTERED arenas would cost, and this lane cannot scatter them.
//
// ⛔ **SO DO NOT "FIX" THE MISMATCH BY SHRINKING `ArenaMapLevelEntries`.** That level's width is an ADDRESS
// SPLIT (`checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts the four fields tile 48 bits); narrowing it to match this arena
// would push bits into L2 and L1 and make every native lane's map deeper to describe a cost this lane does
// not pay.
let UnreservedArenaChunkCount = 64

// An arena's chunk count. ⚠ **RANGED FROM 1 RATHER THAN LEFT A BARE `ParsedInt`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING**:
// `checkArenaMapCoupling` DIVIDES by it, and a type admitting 0 makes that a throwing divide (E3057) — the
// range is where "an arena with no chunks is not a thing" is stated. The upper bound is `i64.max` and not the
// tight `ArenaMapLevelEntries`, because a range bound must be a LITERAL: spelling 8192 here would be a second
// copy of the map level, which is the duplication this whole geometry is written as a derivation to avoid.
// The tight bound is asserted instead, with its reason, in `checkArenaMapCoupling`.
typealias ArenaChunkCount = int(1 to i64.max)

// ⭐ **EVERYTHING AN ARENA'S SIZE DECIDES, CARRIED TOGETHER SO THE PIECES CANNOT BE ASKED SEPARATELY.**
// `buildSlabArenaNew` emits the reservation size and the bitmap's byte count; `buildSlabArenaAllocChunks`
// and `buildSlabArenaFreeChunks` emit the scan bound. Handed the raw chunk count instead, each would derive
// its own — and a builder deriving `bitmapWords` from one geometry while its neighbour derives the scan
// bound from another is an allocator that hands one chunk out twice, with nothing to catch it.
type SlabArenaGeometry
	// Chunks in one arena. Every other figure here is derived from it.
	export let chunkCount as ArenaChunkCount

	// The bitmap that describes them, in machine words.
	export let bitmapWords as ParsedInt

	// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMIT GRANULES IN ONE ARENA — S6's replacement for the WATERMARK, and the reason it had to
	// go.** A watermark says *"`[0, committedChunks)` is committed"*, which is only expressible while commits
	// go one way. The scavenger decommits a granule wherever its chunks happen to have gone free, so the
	// committed set stops being a prefix the moment it runs; a bitmap is the honest shape of the same fact,
	// and it costs 16 words on the reserving lane against the watermark's one.
	export let granuleCount as ParsedInt

	// The bitmap that describes THOSE, in machine words. A CEILING, so the last word may describe fewer than
	// 64 granules — the non-reserving lane has 8 — and every walk bounds itself by `granuleCount` rather than
	// by the word count for exactly that reason.
	export let commitBitmapWords as ParsedInt

	// Where the commit bitmap sits in the metadata chunk: immediately past the chunk bitmap, whose width is
	// itself derived. Carried rather than spelled, because a lane-dependent width means a lane-dependent
	// offset, and an offset computed at each of its four use sites is four chances to describe a different
	// table.
	export let commitBitmapOffset as ByteOffset

	// The arena's own span — the address range its chunk grid covers, measured from the ALIGNED base
	// `__slab_arena_new` starts it at. `__slab_arena_of` tests an address against it.
	export let bytes as ParsedInt

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `__slab_arena_new` ASKS `osReservePages` FOR — `bytes` PLUS THE ROOM THE ALIGN-UP NEEDS.**
	// The base the OS answers is raised to the next commit granule (see `buildSlabArenaNew` for why it must
	// be), which can move it by as much as `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1`; the arena then runs `bytes`
	// from there. Reserving only `bytes` would put the arena's last granule OUTSIDE the reservation, and on
	// the POSIX lanes that granule's commit is a `MAP_FIXED` re-map — a silent clobber of whatever the
	// process had at that address, not a refusal. Carried rather than derived at the one call site so
	// `checkArenaGeometryForLane` can assert the two against each other.
	export let reservationBytes as ParsedInt

	// ⭐ Which lane this geometry IS, carried rather than passed alongside. `checkArenaMapCoupling` asks a
	// DIFFERENT question of each lane, and a geometry travelling beside a separate bool is a pair that can
	// arrive mismatched — a 512 KiB arena checked against the reserving lane's rule would pass the wrong
	// assertion and say nothing.
	export let hasPageReservation as bool

	// The lane's geometry, asked of the one fact that decides it — `IrModule.hasPageReservation`, which is
	// the MECHANISM (is a reservation free?) rather than the target, for the reason its own header gives.
	export static function forReservation(hasPageReservation bool) returns Self
		if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
			return Self.fromChunkCount(ReservedArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation)
		end 'reserves'

		return Self.fromChunkCount(UnreservedArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation)
	end 'forReservation'

	// The derivation, written once for both lanes so a new one cannot arrive with a hand-computed bitmap.
	static function fromChunkCount(chunkCount ArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation bool) returns Self
		let chunks = chunkCount as ParsedInt
		let bitmapWords = chunks shr BitmapBitsPerWordShift
		let granuleCount = chunks shr SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift
		let commitBitmapWords = (granuleCount + BitmapBitsPerWordMask) shr BitmapBitsPerWordShift
		let commitBitmapOffset = ((ArenaBitmapOffset as ParsedInt) + bitmapWords * ArenaWordBytes) as ByteOffset
		let bytes = chunks * SlabPageBytes
		return Self{chunkCount: chunkCount, bitmapWords: bitmapWords, granuleCount: granuleCount, commitBitmapWords: commitBitmapWords, commitBitmapOffset: commitBitmapOffset, bytes: bytes, reservationBytes: bytes + arenaAlignSlackBytes(hasPageReservation), hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation}
	end 'fromChunkCount'
end 'SlabArenaGeometry'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ROOM AN ARENA'S ALIGN-UP NEEDS, AND WHY THE ONE LANE THAT CANNOT AFFORD IT DOES NOT NEED IT.**
// `osReservePages` answers a base aligned to whatever the LANE'S page source hands out and nothing more —
// x64-linux's is 4 KiB, HALF a chunk — so `__slab_arena_new` raises it to the next commit granule and the
// reservation has to be that much longer.
//
// ⭐ **WHERE A RESERVATION IS FREE, SO IS THE SLACK; WHERE IT IS NOT, THERE IS NOTHING TO RAISE.** This is
// `hasPageReservation` read from both sides, and it is ONE fact rather than a lane test wearing two hats:
// the lane that answers *no* answers no because its reservation IS its allocation — `memory.grow`, whose
// unit is `UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes` — and a base handed out in whole grow units is already
// granule-aligned, which is exactly what makes the align-up the identity there. Slack there would be 64 KiB
// of CHARGED linear memory on a 512 KiB arena (+12.5%), against the 5.22 / 11.10 MiB the header measures.
// `checkArenaGeometryForLane` asserts the arithmetic half of that premise rather than trusting it.
function arenaAlignSlackBytes(hasPageReservation bool) returns ParsedInt
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1
	end 'reserves'

	return 0
end 'arenaAlignSlackBytes'

// A bitmap word is a machine word. The shift and the mask are the same fact three times over by necessity
// (a count, the shift that divides by it, the mask that takes a remainder of it), so the pair that CAN be
// derived is derived and the one that cannot is asserted — `checkSlabArenaGeometry`.
let BitmapBitsPerWord = 64
let BitmapBitsPerWordShift = 6
let BitmapBitsPerWordMask = BitmapBitsPerWord - 1

// A machine word, for the COMPILE-TIME size arithmetic here — the bitmap's byte count and each reverse-map
// level's table size. The EMITTED `base + index · 8` is not spelled in this file at all: it is
// `MmRuntime.emitWordSlotAddr`, exported at S2 precisely so a third copy of that arithmetic did not get
// written, and `SlabRuntime`'s `WordBytes` records the same split one layer up. What stays private is the
// LAYOUT constant, because a shared "8" is not a shared fact — these are strides of tables this file owns.
let ArenaWordBytes = 8

// ⭐⭐ **THE UNIT AN ARENA COMMITS IN, AND EVERY REASON FOR THE NUMBER IS A PLATFORM'S.** 64 KiB is Windows'
// ALLOCATION GRANULARITY, it is a whole multiple of every page size the four lanes use — including
// arm64-macOS's **16 KiB**, which is TWICE `SlabPageBytes` and is the constraint that decides this — and it is
// exactly what `MmRuntime`'s arena commits today, so routing the allocator through this layer changes a
// program's resident set by nothing.
//
// ⛔ **A CHUNK-GRANULARITY COMMIT WOULD BE A WRONG ANSWER ON arm64-macOS, NOT MERELY A SMALL ONE.** A chunk is
// 8 KiB; `mmap` REFUSES an address or a length that is not page-aligned, and that lane's page is 16 KiB. So
// the granule is not a batching optimization — it is what makes every committed range page-aligned on every
// lane, which is why it is asserted below rather than tuned.
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes = 65536
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks = SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes shr SlabPageShift

// The two shifts the granule is REACHED by — `chunk >> chunksShift` is its granule, `granule << shift` is its
// byte offset in the arena. A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice by necessity,
// so both are asserted against the widths above (`requireShiftMatchesWidth`) rather than trusted. They exist
// because S6's commit bitmap and its scavenger index BY GRANULE, where the watermark they replaced only ever
// compared chunk counts.
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift = 3
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift = SlabPageShift + SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift

// ⭐ **THE WIDEST PAGE ANY LANE HAS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE GRANULE ABOVE A CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A TASTE.**
// x64 is 4 KiB, **arm64-macOS is 16 KiB** — TWICE a chunk, which is the constraint the granule's own header
// names — and **wasm32's `memory.grow` unit is 64 KiB**, the widest of the four. Every range this file
// reserves or commits is a whole number of granules, so a granule that is not a whole number of the widest
// page is a range `mmap` REFUSES on one lane and a `memory.grow` that silently rounds up on another.
// Asserted rather than left in a comment, which is where the 16 KiB lived until the arena acquired a second
// geometry to check it against.
let LargestTargetPageBytes = 65536

// ⭐⭐ **THE UNIT THE NON-RESERVING LANE'S PAGE SOURCE HANDS OUT — wasm's `memory.grow` PAGE.** It is 64 KiB
// by the wasm specification, and `StdToWasm.emitOsAllocPages` answers `prevPages shl 16`, so EVERY base that
// lane can hand `__slab_arena_new` is already a whole multiple of it. That is the premise
// `arenaAlignSlackBytes` rests on when it gives that lane ZERO slack, and `checkArenaReservationSlack` asks
// it as arithmetic rather than trusting it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS NOT `LargestTargetPageBytes`, THOUGH THE TWO ARE THE SAME NUMBER TODAY — one name for two
// facts is how the slack check would come to FAIL OPEN.** That one is a MAXIMUM over every lane; this one is
// ONE lane's unit, and the two checks that read them want OPPOSITE divisibility: a granule must be a whole
// number of the widest page, and the grow unit must be a whole number of granules. Written as one constant
// the pair holds only while wasm HAPPENS to be the widest lane — add a lane with a wider page and
// `granuleSplitsAPage` correctly forces the granule up with it, a max-based slack test still passes, and
// wasm's unchanged 64 KiB base stops being granule-aligned on the one lane that reserves no slack to absorb
// it. `checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts this one cannot exceed the maximum, which is what keeps a stale pair
// from surviving a lane change quietly.
let UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes = 65536

// ⭐ **CHUNK 0 IS THE ARENA'S OWN METADATA, so its bit is never free and it is never handed out.** That
// costs one chunk in 8192 (0.01%) and buys two things: the metadata needs no second mapping, and a returned
// chunk address is never the arena base, so the two can never be confused by a caller that holds one.
let ArenaMetadataChunks = 1

// Where the chunk bitmap sits in the metadata chunk: past the arena-list link, which occupies the first
// word. The granule COMMIT bitmap follows it at the geometry's derived `commitBitmapOffset`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE COMMITTED SET IS A BITMAP AND NOT A WATERMARK, AND THAT IS THE SCAVENGER'S PRECONDITION.** One
// word saying *"`[0, committedChunks)` is committed"* is only true while commits go one way. A scavenger
// decommits a granule wherever the chunks happen to have gone free — in the MIDDLE of the range a watermark
// claims — so the committed set stops being a prefix, the mark reports a decommitted granule as committed,
// and the next claim inside it hands the caller pages Windows has made INACCESSIBLE. The bitmap costs 16
// words against one on the reserving lane, and one word on the other.
let ArenaBitmapOffset = 8 as ByteOffset

// --- The reverse map's geometry ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THREE LEVELS OVER THE PAGE INDEX, AND THE SPLIT IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN TASTE.** An address is
// `[ L1 | L2 | L3 | page offset ]`, and `checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts the four widths sum to
// `ArenaMapAddressBits` — so a change to any one of them fails the next compile instead of silently
// indexing past a table. The bottom field is `SlabPageShift`, READ from the class table rather than spelled:
// the L3 index IS the page index, which is the one place a second spelling of the page shift would bite.
//
// 48 bits is the canonical user-address width the compiler must cover: x64 user mode is 47 bits and arm64 is 48, so
// the mask is a no-op on every address a program can hold and a containment guarantee on any that is not.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVING LANE'S ARENA IS SIZED TO THIS LEVEL, WHICH IS THE COUPLING THE 2 MiB ARENA BROKE.** An
// L3 table covers `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages — 64 MiB of address space — and `ReservedArenaChunkCount` IS
// that entry count, so one arena is one table's span — a DERIVATION rather than a coincidence, so the two
// cannot drift. An arena that starts on a 64 MiB boundary
// spends one 64 KiB table (0.1% of the arena); one that straddles spends two (0.2%). The header says what an
// ALIGNED reservation would buy and what it would cost.
//
// ⚠ **THE NON-RESERVING LANE IS 1:128 INSTEAD, AND `checkSlabArenaGeometry` CHECKS THAT RATIO RATHER THAN THE
// IDENTITY.** `UnreservedArenaChunkCount` records why the cost is still ~0.1% there — successive arenas are
// ADJACENT in linear memory, so one table serves many — and what the ratio must remain for that argument to
// hold: a WHOLE number of arenas per table. An arena that did not divide the level would put one table's
// entries under two arenas' worth of straddling, which is the 12.5% the contiguity is what avoids.
let ArenaMapAddressBits = 48
let ArenaMapLevelBits = 13
let ArenaMapLevelEntries = 8192
let ArenaMapLevelMask = ArenaMapLevelEntries - 1
let ArenaMapL1Bits = 9
let ArenaMapL1Entries = 512
let ArenaMapL1Mask = ArenaMapL1Entries - 1
let ArenaMapL2Shift = SlabPageShift + ArenaMapLevelBits
let ArenaMapL1Shift = ArenaMapL2Shift + ArenaMapLevelBits

// ⭐ **THE MISS ANSWER, NAMED.** `__slab_arena_map_get` answering 0 is not a defaulted return — it is the
// OS-DIRECT SENTINEL, and the layer above reads it as *"this pointer was never registered as belonging to a
// span, so it came straight from the OS and goes straight back"*. It is spelled here so the producer (the
// three miss blocks) and the future consumer cannot disagree about which value carries that meaning.
export let ArenaMapNoSpan = 0

// --- Entry points ---

export let SlabArenaAllocChunksName = b"__slab_arena_alloc_chunks"
export let SlabArenaFreeChunksName = b"__slab_arena_free_chunks"
export let SlabArenaOfName = b"__slab_arena_of"
export let SlabArenaScavengeName = b"__slab_arena_scavenge"
export let SlabArenaMapSetName = b"__slab_arena_map_set"
export let SlabArenaMapGetName = b"__slab_arena_map_get"

// ⚠ **THE OS-ALLOCATION GUARD IS THE FAMILY'S AND ITS CALLERS ARE NOT.** It guards `osAllocPages` rather
// than the arena — the OS-direct path in `SlabRuntime` and the GT record carve in `SchedRuntime` are its
// other two callers, and that second one is minted off `usesGt` rather than `usesHeap`. What makes that
// harmless is that its body reads no `.data` word: it is a compare and an exit.
export let SlabAssertOsAllocName = b"__slab_assert_os_alloc"

// The two `.data` words this family owns. Both `__`-prefixed, so no user name can collide and neither
// reaches a spec golden. ⭐ Their zero initializer IS the initial state, not a default: `list == 0` means
// "no arena mapped yet" and `map l1 == 0` means "nothing registered yet", and both are what the first call
// on each path tests for.
//
// ⭐ **THEY ARE LAID OUT WHERE A SURVIVING ARENA BODY NAMES THEM** (`DataReach.walked`), which is what keeps
// a program that cannot allocate from carrying them: dead-function elimination sweeps every arena body such
// a program never calls, and the words go with them.
//
// ⚠ **THEY ARE EXPORTED BECAUSE A `__Raw` ADDRESS ROW NAMES THEM** — `slabArenaListAddr` and
// `slabArenaMapL1Addr` lower to a `globalAddr` on these very constants (`LowerMaxonToStd`), so the label the
// image lays out and the label the tier body reads cannot be two spellings.
// ⭐ **THE TIER FILE THIS FAMILY'S EIGHT ENTRY POINTS ARE WRITTEN IN.** `StdlibSource` reads it to declare
// the reach edge the from-`main` walk cannot see; it is here rather than there because which file holds the
// arena is this family's fact, and a second spelling of the name in the reader is how the two come to name
// different files while everything still compiles.
export let SlabArenaTierFileName = "SlabArena.maxon"

export let SlabArenaListLabel = b"__slab_arena_list"
export let SlabArenaMapL1Label = b"__slab_arena_map_l1"

// The arena family's `.data` slots. Both are read only by tier bodies, so each is laid out exactly where a
// surviving body names it (`DataReach.walked`); no gate is asked here. They do not go through
// `pushRuntimeGlobal` because their discipline is `__slab_lock`, held by the caller
// (`runtime/SlabArena.maxon`'s header), which is no `MultiMSharing` class.
export function slabArenaRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(SlabArenaListLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0, reach: DataReach.walked))
	out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(SlabArenaMapL1Label), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0, reach: DataReach.walked))
	return out
end 'slabArenaRuntimeGlobals'

// Install the one entry point of the page layer that is still built here — `__slab_arena_map_get`. The
// other eight are `runtime/SlabArena.maxon` source and arrive through the ordinary pipeline.
//
// ⭐ **THE GEOMETRY CHECK RUNS BEFORE THE GATE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** The compiles it must NOT skip are
// the heapless ones: a geometry that stops satisfying its own rule has to fail the first compile after the
// change, and a check that only runs when a program happens to allocate is a check that reports the
// breakage to whoever comes next. It is two dozen integer comparisons against a compile that emits
// megabytes, and a flag is what would let it stop running.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT CHECKS BOTH LANES' GEOMETRIES, NOT THIS BUILD'S.** A wasm-only breakage found only by a wasm
// compile is the same skipped check one axis over, and both geometries are compile-time constants.
//
// ⛔ **THE CHECK IS OVER THIS FILE'S OWN COPY OF THE GEOMETRY, AND THE TIER RESTATES THE SEED CONSTANTS
// RATHER THAN SHARING THEM** — no name crosses the tier boundary. What it asserts is that the DERIVATION
// loses nothing on either lane, which is the half a drifted seed cannot fake: an arena whose bitmap is short
// or whose granule overruns it fails the next compile, on both sides, because the tier writes the same
// derivation over the same seeds.
export function installSlabArena(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	checkSlabArenaGeometry()

	if not usage.usesSlabArena 'noArena'
		return
	end 'noArena'

	buildSlabArenaMapGet(module)
end 'installSlabArena'

// ⭐⭐ **THE GEOMETRY IS INTERNALLY COHERENT — asserted, not trusted.** `checkSlabClassTable` beside it makes
// the same argument for the size-class ladder and states it in full: a table whose source changed shape
// produces a working-looking allocator that indexes past its own bounds. Every number below is either
// derived from another or checked against the one it must agree with; the file declares nothing that agrees
// with nothing.
//
// It panics rather than returning a verdict, for that check's reason: a broken arena geometry cannot
// produce a working allocator, so there is no caller who could sensibly continue.
function checkSlabArenaGeometry()
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("bitmap word", shift: BitmapBitsPerWordShift, width: BitmapBitsPerWord)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("map level", shift: ArenaMapLevelBits, width: ArenaMapLevelEntries)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("map L1", shift: ArenaMapL1Bits, width: ArenaMapL1Entries)

	// The commit granule is a whole number of chunks (so the shift that derives its chunk count loses
	// nothing). A fact about the granule alone, so it is asked once rather than once per arena geometry.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks * SlabPageBytes != SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes 'granuleNotWholeChunks'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes} bytes is not a whole number of {SlabPageBytes}-byte chunks")
	end 'granuleNotWholeChunks'

	// The two shifts a granule is reached by. `emitCommitGranulesFor` divides a CHUNK index by the first and
	// `buildSlabArenaScavenge` multiplies a GRANULE index by the second, so a shift that stopped matching its
	// width would commit one granule and decommit another.
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("commit granule chunks", shift: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("commit granule bytes", shift: SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes)

	// ⭐⭐ **A GRANULE'S CHUNKS MUST ALL LIE IN ONE BITMAP WORD, which is what makes the scavenger's
	// "is this granule entirely free?" ONE masked compare rather than a nested loop.** It holds with 56 bits
	// of headroom today (8 chunks in a 64-bit word) and is asserted because widening the granule is exactly
	// the change that would quietly turn that compare into a wrong answer about the granule's second half.
	//
	// ⚠ **`<=` IS THE WHOLE TEST ONLY BECAUSE BOTH ARE POWERS OF TWO, and that premise is established two
	// lines up rather than assumed here.** A granule's first chunk is `g · granuleChunks`, so its bit
	// position is `(g · granuleChunks) mod BitmapBitsPerWord`, which is a multiple of `granuleChunks` — and a
	// field of `granuleChunks` bits starting at a multiple of `granuleChunks` fits inside the word exactly
	// when `granuleChunks` DIVIDES the word width. `requireShiftMatchesWidth` above proves the granule is a
	// power of two and this proves it is no wider than the word, and for two powers of two those two
	// together are divisibility. A granule of, say, 24 chunks would pass `<=` and still straddle.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks > BitmapBitsPerWord 'granuleSpansBitmapWords'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunks does not fit one {BitmapBitsPerWord}-bit bitmap word, so the scavenger's all-free test would miss the chunks past the first word")
	end 'granuleSpansBitmapWords'

	// ⭐ **AND THE GRANULE IS A WHOLE NUMBER OF THE WIDEST PAGE ANY LANE HAS.** Every range this file names to
	// the OS is a whole number of granules, so this is what makes each one page-aligned everywhere at once —
	// `mmap` REFUSES a length that is not, and `memory.grow` silently rounds one up.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes mod LargestTargetPageBytes != 0 'granuleSplitsAPage'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes} bytes is not a whole number of {LargestTargetPageBytes}-byte pages, so a committed range would split a page on the widest-page lane")
	end 'granuleSplitsAPage'

	// The non-reserving lane's grow unit IS one of the lanes' page sizes, so it can never be wider than the
	// widest of them. Asserted because the two constants hold the same number today and
	// `checkArenaReservationSlack` reads THIS one: a grow unit left stale by a lane change would make that
	// check answer for a page source no lane has, which is the shape its own header calls failing open.
	if UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes > LargestTargetPageBytes 'growUnitAboveWidestPage'
		panic("slab arena: the non-reserving lane's {UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes}-byte grow unit is wider than the {LargestTargetPageBytes}-byte widest page any lane has, so one of the two is stale")
	end 'growUnitAboveWidestPage'

	// `__slab_arena_new` clears the metadata chunks' bits by storing `-(1 shl ArenaMetadataChunks)` into
	// bitmap word 0, which is only the right answer while those bits all live in word 0. A fact about the
	// metadata reservation and the word width, and about neither arena's size.
	if ArenaMetadataChunks >= BitmapBitsPerWord 'metadataSpansWords'
		panic("slab arena: {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunks do not fit in one {BitmapBitsPerWord}-bit bitmap word")
	end 'metadataSpansWords'

	// `__slab_arena_new` commits ONE granule and then writes the metadata into it, so the metadata chunks must
	// lie inside that first granule. Short, and the arena would initialise its own bitmap through a pointer
	// into uncommitted — on Windows and on a `PROT_NONE` POSIX reservation alike, INACCESSIBLE — memory.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks < ArenaMetadataChunks 'metadataPastFirstGranule'
		panic("slab arena: {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunk(s) do not fit in the first commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunk(s)")
	end 'metadataPastFirstGranule'

	// The four fields of an address must TILE it. Short, and a page's worth of addresses at the top of the
	// space would index nothing; long, and the L1 index would run past its table.
	let mappedBits = SlabPageShift + ArenaMapLevelBits + ArenaMapLevelBits + ArenaMapL1Bits

	if mappedBits != ArenaMapAddressBits 'addressBitsDisagree'
		panic("slab arena map: {SlabPageShift} + {ArenaMapLevelBits} + {ArenaMapLevelBits} + {ArenaMapL1Bits} is {mappedBits} bits, not the {ArenaMapAddressBits} the map claims to cover")
	end 'addressBitsDisagree'

	checkArenaGeometryForLane(SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(true))
	checkArenaGeometryForLane(SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(false))
end 'checkSlabArenaGeometry'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE LANE'S ARENA MUST SATISFY, ASKED OF BOTH — because a rule that stops applying to the lane
// nobody happens to be compiling is the "check that never runs" one signature up, wearing a target's
// clothes.** Every panic here names the lane, since the two geometries produce the same sentence with
// different numbers and a reader who cannot tell them apart has to re-derive which arena broke.
function checkArenaGeometryForLane(geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	let lane = arenaLaneName(geometry.hasPageReservation)

	// The bitmap's width is the chunk count shifted down by a word's bit count, and a LOSSY shift would leave
	// the top chunks with no bit at all — free forever, and handed out by nobody. The scan would still work,
	// which is what makes it worth asserting.
	if geometry.bitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord != geometry.chunkCount as ParsedInt 'bitmapShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.bitmapWords} bitmap words describe {geometry.bitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord} chunks, not the arena's {geometry.chunkCount}")
	end 'bitmapShort'

	// The granule is a whole divisor of the arena — without it, the last granule's commit would run past the
	// reservation and the OS would refuse it, and the derived granule count would describe fewer chunks than
	// the arena has. (That the granule is itself a whole number of chunks is asked of the granule alone, one
	// signature up.)
	if geometry.granuleCount * SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks != geometry.chunkCount as ParsedInt 'granuleOverrunsArena'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.granuleCount} commit granules describe {geometry.granuleCount * SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunks, not the arena's {geometry.chunkCount}")
	end 'granuleOverrunsArena'

	// The commit bitmap is a CEILING, so it may describe MORE granules than the arena has — never fewer, which
	// would leave the top granules with no commit bit and the claim path re-committing a live range on every
	// call that reached them.
	if geometry.commitBitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord < geometry.granuleCount 'commitBitmapShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.commitBitmapWords} commit-bitmap words describe {geometry.commitBitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord} granules, fewer than the arena's {geometry.granuleCount}")
	end 'commitBitmapShort'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SMALLEST ARENA MUST STILL SATISFY THE LARGEST RUN THIS FILE ASKS OF ITSELF, AND SHRINKING AN
	// ARENA IS EXACTLY HOW THAT STOPS BEING TRUE.** The reverse map's level tables are allocated out of an
	// arena, and an L2/L3 level is the biggest single request the page layer makes. An arena too small for it
	// does not fail to compile: `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` REFUSES the request at run time with
	// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable`, on one lane, in a build whose native twin is perfect. It subsumes the
	// weaker "an arena has any payload chunks at all", which is why that is not asked separately.
	let largestMapTableRun = arenaChunksFor(ArenaMapLevelEntries * ArenaWordBytes)
	let payloadChunks = (geometry.chunkCount as ParsedInt) - ArenaMetadataChunks

	if largestMapTableRun > payloadChunks 'mapTableRunUnsatisfiable'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): a {largestMapTableRun}-chunk reverse-map level does not fit the {payloadChunks} chunks an arena has after its {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunk(s), so `__slab_arena_map_ensure` would abort on the first lookup")
	end 'mapTableRunUnsatisfiable'

	// The list link and the two bitmaps are the whole of the metadata chunk — the commit bitmap is last, so
	// its end is what can overflow — and nothing may silently start overlapping the payload of chunk 0 if
	// either widens.
	let metadataBytes = (geometry.commitBitmapOffset as ParsedInt) + geometry.commitBitmapWords * ArenaWordBytes

	if metadataBytes > ArenaMetadataChunks * SlabPageBytes 'metadataOverflowsChunk'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {metadataBytes} bytes of metadata do not fit in {ArenaMetadataChunks} chunk(s) of {SlabPageBytes}")
	end 'metadataOverflowsChunk'

	checkArenaReservationSlack(geometry, lane: lane)
	checkArenaMapCoupling(geometry.chunkCount, hasPageReservation: geometry.hasPageReservation, lane: lane)
end 'checkArenaGeometryForLane'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVATION MUST STILL HOLD THE ARENA AFTER THE ALIGN-UP HAS MOVED IT — asked as TWO rules,
// because the two lanes satisfy it for opposite reasons and one rule covering both would hold vacuously for
// whichever lane it was not written for.** Short by a byte and the arena's last granule is committed OUTSIDE
// the reservation, which on the POSIX lanes is a `MAP_FIXED` re-map over whatever the process had there — a
// clobber, not a refusal, and the one failure this geometry could produce that no abort would ever name.
function checkArenaReservationSlack(geometry SlabArenaGeometry, lane String)
	if not geometry.hasPageReservation 'noSlackToSpend'
		// The lane pays for its reservation, so it reserves EXACTLY the arena — which is only sound because
		// its page source hands out whole `UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes` units and a granule divides one,
		// making the align-up the identity. That divisibility is the premise, and it is arithmetic.
		if UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes mod SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes != 0 'growUnitIsNotWholeGranules'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): a page source handing out {UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes}-byte units does not answer a whole number of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes}-byte commit granules, so `__slab_arena_new`'s align-up could move a base this lane reserves no room for")
		end 'growUnitIsNotWholeGranules'

		if geometry.reservationBytes != geometry.bytes 'unreservedLaneOverReserves'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): reserving {geometry.reservationBytes} bytes for a {geometry.bytes}-byte arena charges this lane for slack its already-aligned base never uses")
		end 'unreservedLaneOverReserves'

		return
	end 'noSlackToSpend'

	// A lane with reservations pays only address space, so it buys the worst case outright: an align-up can
	// move the base by as much as one granule less a byte.
	if geometry.reservationBytes - geometry.bytes < SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1 'slackTooShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): a {geometry.reservationBytes}-byte reservation leaves {geometry.reservationBytes - geometry.bytes} bytes of slack for a {geometry.bytes}-byte arena, fewer than the {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1} an align-up to a commit granule can consume")
	end 'slackTooShort'
end 'checkArenaReservationSlack'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ARENA↔MAP-TABLE RATIO, WHICH IS THE ONE INVARIANT THE TWO LANES DO NOT SHARE — SO IT IS ASKED AS
// TWO RULES RATHER THAN AS ONE THAT HOLDS VACUOUSLY.** Where a reservation is free the arena IS one L3
// table's span (the identity `ReservedArenaChunkCount` is written as), and the map costs 0.1–0.2%. Where it
// is not, the arena is deliberately SMALLER than a table and what has to hold instead is that a whole number
// of them tile one — which is what makes `UnreservedArenaChunkCount`'s contiguity argument arithmetic rather
// than a hope. Collapsing the pair into the weaker rule would let the reserving lane silently shrink to an
// eighth of its table and lose the 0.1% without anything noticing.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE CHUNK COUNT RATHER THAN THE GEOMETRY, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.** The `mod` needs
// a divisor whose type excludes 0, and passing the ranged field through a PARAMETER is what supplies that
// — `ArenaMapLevelEntries mod geometry.chunkCount` reaches E3057 instead. Do not "simplify" this back to
// taking the geometry.
function checkArenaMapCoupling(chunkCount ArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation bool, lane String)
	if not hasPageReservation 'tilesATable'
		if ArenaMapLevelEntries mod chunkCount != 0 'arenaStraddlesTables'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): an L3 table's {ArenaMapLevelEntries} pages are not a whole number of {chunkCount}-chunk arenas, so adjacent arenas would straddle tables and the map's cost is not the ~0.1% `UnreservedArenaChunkCount` claims")
		end 'arenaStraddlesTables'

		return
	end 'tilesATable'

	if chunkCount != ArenaMapLevelEntries 'arenaIsNotOneTable'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): an arena of {chunkCount} chunks is not one L3 table's {ArenaMapLevelEntries} pages, so a table would describe more address space than the arena it was allocated for")
	end 'arenaIsNotOneTable'
end 'checkArenaMapCoupling'

// Which arena a panic above is talking about. The two geometries produce the same sentences with different
// numbers, and "512 KiB, not 64 MiB" is only readable once the reader is told which one was expected.
function arenaLaneName(hasPageReservation bool) returns String
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return "reserving lanes"
	end 'reserves'

	return "the non-reserving lane"
end 'arenaLaneName'

// A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice, by necessity — this file has four
// such pairs, and the emitted code reaches each pair through whichever half its own expression happened to
// name. `checkSlabClassTable` asserts exactly this about `SlabPageShift`/`SlabPageBytes` and states the
// reason in full: a change to one half leaves the other silently describing a different geometry.
function requireShiftMatchesWidth(what String, shift ParsedInt, width ParsedInt)
	let produced = 1 shl shift

	if produced != width 'disagrees'
		panic("slab arena: {what}: 1 shl {shift} is {produced}, not {width}")
	end 'disagrees'
end 'requireShiftMatchesWidth'

// Chunks a byte count occupies, rounded up. The reverse map's three table sizes are the only caller, and
// they are compile-time constants — this runs in the COMPILER, so the runtime pays a `const` and no divide.
function arenaChunksFor(bytes ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	return (bytes + SlabPageBytes - 1) / SlabPageBytes
end 'arenaChunksFor'

// ⭐ **THE LARGEST RUN `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` CAN EVER SATISFY ON A LANE.** Exported because the OBJECT
// layer is what makes the requests that have to fit — a span's chunk run and the allocator's own state
// region — and it had no way to ask. `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asks it of BOTH lanes for
// `checkArenaGeometryForLane`'s reason: a request too large is refused at RUN TIME with
// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable`, on one lane, in a build whose native twin is perfect.
export function slabArenaAllocatableChunks(hasPageReservation bool) returns ParsedInt
	return (SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(hasPageReservation).chunkCount as ParsedInt) - ArenaMetadataChunks
end 'slabArenaAllocatableChunks'

// The address of one map slot: `tableBase + ((addr shr shift) and mask) * 8`. ONE spelling, shared by the
// SIX index computations — `ensure` indexes L1, L2 and L3, and `get` repeats all three — which is what
// makes "the L2 index is bits 26..38" a single statement rather than six. (The L1 TABLE's own address is a
// `globalAddr` in both, not an index, which is why there are six and not eight.)
function emitMapSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tableBase ValueId, addr ValueId, shift ParsedInt, mask ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let shiftConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: shift, ids: ids)
	let raw = emitBin(module, block: block, a: addr, b: shiftConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	let maskConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: mask, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: raw, b: maskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: tableBase, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitMapSlotAddr'
// **THE SPAN REGISTERED FOR THE PAGE HOLDING `ptr`, or `ArenaMapNoSpan`** — emitted INTO the caller's
// blocks, answering in `spanId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE MISS IS AN ANSWER, NOT A DEFAULT.** Three levels can be absent, and every one of them means the
// same thing: nothing registered this address, so it did not come from a span — it came straight from the
// OS and the layer above releases it that way. `ArenaMapNoSpan` names that meaning so the three miss edges
// and the consumer cannot disagree about it. This is the one place in this file a zero return is not a
// sentinel standing in for a failure, and it is spelled out because it reads exactly like one.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE FIRST QUESTION EVERY FREE ASKS, AND SINCE EC8 `__slab_free` ASKS IT WITHOUT A CALL.** The
// walk is four loads and three tests over tables nothing ever overwrites — call-free by construction — so a
// frame around it was pure overhead on the path of every free in the language. `__slab_free_to_span`'s note
// makes the same argument for the push that follows it.
//
// It takes no lock and needs none even once the mutation paths take one: a published level pointer is never
// overwritten, so a reader either sees 0 or sees a fully zeroed table.
export function emitSlabArenaMapGet(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ptr ValueId, spanId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var atL2 = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var atL3 = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var atPage = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var miss = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(spanId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let l1SlotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.globalAddr(l1SlotAddr, label: SlabArenaMapL1Label))
	let l1 = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: l1SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: cur, pointer: l1, present: atL2, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let l2SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL2, tableBase: l1, addr: ptr, shift: ArenaMapL1Shift, mask: ArenaMapL1Mask, ids: ids)
	let l2 = emitLoadField(module, block: atL2, record: l2SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: atL2, pointer: l2, present: atL3, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let l3SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL3, tableBase: l2, addr: ptr, shift: ArenaMapL2Shift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)
	let l3 = emitLoadField(module, block: atL3, record: l3SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: atL3, pointer: l3, present: atPage, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let pageSlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atPage, tableBase: l3, addr: ptr, shift: SlabPageShift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)
	let span = emitLoadField(module, block: atPage, record: pageSlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(atPage, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	atPage.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(span)))

	let absent = emitConst(module, block: miss, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(miss, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	miss.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(absent)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabArenaMapGet'

// `__slab_arena_map_get(ptr) -> span` — the same walk behind a frame, for `__slab_drain_remote`, whose
// replay loop asks it once per queued slot. That loop is a MISS path, so a frame there costs a program
// nothing it would notice and saves the walk being written into the emitted image a second time.
function buildSlabArenaMapGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaMapGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0 as ValueId

	let span = ids.mint()
	var answered = emitSlabArenaMapGet(module, func: func, cur: entry, ptr: ptr, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"map")
	module.setTerminator(answered, op: StdOp.ret(span))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaMapGet'

// Continue into `present` when `pointer` is non-zero, else into `absent`. The lookup's three level tests are
// the same three ops, and writing them out per level is how one of the three ends up comparing the wrong
// value.
function emitBranchOnPresent(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pointer ValueId, present IrBlock, absent IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let uncommitted = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: pointer, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: absent.id, elseBlockId: present.id))
end 'emitBranchOnPresent'
